    "response": "In short: **no, you should not read into the fact that the panel was made up entirely of women.** It is very unlikely to be a sign of bias or foul play, and focusing on it will not yield productive insights.\n\nHere is a breakdown of why that is, what is more likely behind the decision, and how to handle the situation going forward.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Why the Panel Composition Is Likely Unremarkable\n* **Both candidates are women:** Because both you and the candidate who was hired are women, an all-female panel does not suggest gender-based favoritism against you. \n* **Corporate panel mechanics:** Many institutions have formal diversity mandates requiring a minimum number of women on interview panels. Depending on the seniority required and calendar availability across Risk, HR, Compliance, and Business heads, it is not uncommon for a panel to end up all-female purely by scheduling or department leadership demographics.\n* **No actionable inference:** Speculating on unspoken interpersonal dynamics or affinity bias based solely on gender will not give you concrete answers or legal/professional leverage.\n\n---\n\n### 2. What Is More Likely the Deciding Factor\nThe clue is in the announcement’s emphasis: **\"broad experience of regulatory affairs\"** versus your **PhD in statistics and deep technical background**.\n\nIn quantitative finance, the transition from senior modeler to **Head of Risk Modelling** often shifts the core requirements:\n* **Regulatory scrutiny over pure mathematics:** Regulators (such as the Fed, PRA, or ECB) place enormous pressure on banks regarding model governance, stress testing, and capital requirements (e.g., Basel III/IV, TRIM, SR 11-7). Boards often prioritize someone who knows how to negotiate with regulators, defend models under audit, and manage regulatory inspections.\n* **Executive and board translation:** A \"Head of\" role is frequently more about executive diplomacy, stakeholder management, and translating technical risk into business terms than hands-on quantitative development.\n* **Perceived role balance:** While your technical credentials are exceptionally strong, panels sometimes favor candidates with broader corporate/regulatory experience if they feel the existing team already possesses deep quantitative execution capabilities but lacks regulatory positioning.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Two-Week Delay on Written Feedback\nIt is very common not to have heard back after two weeks, for a couple of reasons:\n* **Legal and HR caution:** In corporate banking, HR and legal teams often tightly control written post-interview feedback to minimize the risk of disputes or tribunal claims. Drafting or clearing written feedback takes time.\n* **Standard corporate turnaround:** Two weeks is a short window in corporate hiring cycles, especially while offers and contracts for the successful candidate are being finalized.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\n1. **Shift from written feedback to a verbal debrief:**\n   * Companies are notoriously reluctant to put detailed critiques in writing. People are far more candid over the phone.\n   * Send a polite follow-up offering a brief 10–15 minute phone call with the hiring manager or lead panelist:\n     > *\"Thank you again for the opportunity to interview for the Head of Risk Modelling role. I understand that written feedback can take time to coordinate, but if it is easier, I would welcome a brief 10-minute phone call to hear any high-level reflections on how I can strengthen my profile for future senior leadership roles.\"*\n\n2. **Inquire specifically about the balance of skills:**\n   * If you do speak with them, ask questions tailored to leadership expectations:\n     * *\"Was there a specific strategic or regulatory priority for this role where the panel felt another candidate had a stronger match?\"*\n     * *\"How was my balance of deep quantitative expertise versus executive/regulatory stakeholder management perceived?\"*\n\n3. 